this is steve uh i'm starting this  series called steve summers sessions  where i'm going to answer questions that  you will send to me i hope uh via  twitter  uh hashtags ask steve  and uh once a week i'm going to answer  them here in the month of june and  perhaps beyond if the if there's enough  interest  and i'm going to try to answer two  questions every week  this week i'm going to answer two of the  questions that were submitted to me  through twitter the first one is  how do you improve reading comprehension  i find this a real problem  all right now  my most recent language is romanian so  i'll describe basically how i've gone  through it in the case of romanian but  it applies to all languages first of all  the bottom line as people like to say  in improving your reading comprehension  is to read as much as possible  so much in language learning comes down  to  massive exposure  massive involvement massive time  commitment massive immersion things of  that nature but  obviously to read the more interesting  the content the better your retention is  going to be if you are interested  in the subject matter then you'll have a  better chance of understanding  the subject matter if you are familiar  with the subject matter this has been  proven many times  more necessarily than than studying a  vocabulary list the number one issue is  are you familiar with the subject are  you interested in the subject okay now  when we are learning a language the  problem often is that to begin with  uh most of the content you're that you  would be interested in is too difficult  for you and the content that is  available for you the beginner material  is not interesting so there's no there's  no sense of  of involvement or attachment so the in  an initial period you have to go through  reading things that are not interesting  now as much as possible  listen to what you read and read what  you listen to  um  you know i once bought a russian book on  history and the people who produced it  somewhere in the states made a big deal  of the fact that their audio didn't  correspond to the texts well why would  you possibly do that  um when you are listening and when you  are reading you're trying to pay  attention to words and phrases you're  trying to get them sort of uh ingrained  in your in in your mind and so therefore  the the repetition that you would get by  reading something and then listening to  it and noticing  uh certain structures or certain words  even though other  parts of what you're listening to will  remain unclear for quite a while but as  you're able to nail down a few then  you're able to focus your attention on  the others so  read what you listen to listen to what  you read  the next thing is when you're starting  out in the language you in my experience  you have to listen often and read often  to the same material  and as you progress that is less and  less the case and obviously when you  begin uh you're dealing with short  passages in terms of the length of time  maybe 20 30 40 seconds long  uh even though you might stack three or  four of them  uh together and then review the first  one again you're still dealing with  short passages and as you progress the  passages become  longer  uh  i  like obviously working initially on the  computer where i have access to online  dictionaries  saves me the time of looking things up  or  you know in the case of sometimes there  are people who like bilingual texts  asimil has translations personally i  don't like them personally i prefer to  pick out the words and phrases  uh within the text that i'm reading  try to get a handle on what they mean  and over time i accumulate more and more  of these words and phrases and so i'm  able to make sense of more and more of  the text so my advice on reading  comprehension is a fairly long period of  time where you're working with a  computer now again in my case i uh  initially work on the computer just to  look up words because it's faster but  when i read it the second or third time  or sometimes i just go through very  quickly looking at words and then i read  it  at my ledger on my ipad or iphone so we  have these tools that enables us enable  us to do our reading away from the  computer however  one last thing on reading comprehension  you have to get to where you can read  books you eventually have to develop  that sense of independence from the  computer and even if you i when i read  on paper when i read books i never look  up words because this is a different  thing and i think it's very important in  one's sort of progression towards being  able to read well that you actually take  on a book and i've often likened this to  climbing mount everest the first  full-length book you read in the  language away from the computer is a  major achievement and ultimately if you  want to have good reading comprehension  you need to read books and i'll just  finish off by saying that reading  comprehension is very important for  accumulating vocabulary for getting a  sense of the language finding things  that you notice about the language so  reading a lot  both with the computer initially and  then increasingly away from the computer  is very important for your reading  comprehension and your language skills  generally now the second question i got  was  how do i get from input to output  many of you know that i stress input  listening and reading as being a very  cost effective and time effective and  portable efficient way of  improving your language skills but all  of us our  eventual goal is to speak the language  and in many cases to speak it well so  how do you get from input to output all  right first thing to remember is it to  some extent it depends on your goals if  all you want to output is hello my name  is so and so  could you please tell me where the bus  station is then you can do that fairly  soon  but if our goal is to develop the  ability to have meaningful conversations  with people who share  interests with us so that we can join  with people socially and go out and  participate in conversations and speak  then that's a different order so  when it comes to output the only way to  get good at output eventually is to  speak and write and when you start  speaking you're going to have difficulty  you are going to stumble you won't  remember all the words that you thought  you could remember  so  my advice here is focus initially on a  lot of input  build up your vocabulary build up your  familiarity because  your input skills in my opinion are very  important for your output skills if you  want to have a meaningful conversation  you have to understand what the other  person is saying it's not enough to trot  out a few sentences and then face a  deluge of  the target language back at you and not  understand what the person said  so  i prefer to build up my  call them passive skills through a lot  of listening and reading and building up  my vocabulary  so that when i start speaking  a i have a chance because i understand  what the person is saying  i hope  and and b i can already get involved in  some meaningful conversations  it's just as as was the case with  reading comprehension you have to push  yourself to a level where  what you're talking about just as was  the case with reading comprehension what  you're reading is actually meaningful to  you  you don't want or personally i don't  want to be limited to artificial  situations such as the role playing that  takes place in classrooms i want to get  to where i'm having meaningful  conversations so i put a lot of emphasis  on building up that passive vocabulary  that comprehension capability through  very heavy you know investment of time  in input now when i start to speak i  know i'm going to stumble  so i don't stop my input activities the  speaking the output is often a way to  discover where my gaps are i didn't  notice certain things i'm not using the  subjunctive uh correctly because  uh  and when i went to speak i realized that  i made a mistake or i was looking for a  word then i'm more attentive to that  when i'm listening and reading but uh  you just have to find to get really good  at output you have to find more and more  opportunity to speak some people are  willing to uh you know have the  discipline to write if you have that  discipline writing is excellent and if  you are writing if you are preparing for  an exam or for uh  you know where you're going to be tested  then absolutely you should write because  when you're writing you have time to  think about what you're going to say you  can look things up you can look up the  correct tense you can look up the  correct ending  and you you're more likely to use  some of your recently learned words in  writing than you are in speaking and so  once you have some practice doing that  in writing then  you're more likely to start using some  of these words when you speak but  ultimately you just have to speak a lot  another thing about outfit is don't  worry about how you sound don't worry  about your pronunciation don't worry  about the mistakes you make focus on  communication  and that's another reason why  in seeking out people to talk to  uh don't  i'm not a fan of the idea that you  arrive in some town and  go up to people and say here i am i'm  learning your language and would you  mind talking to me to me that's not a  meaningful situation i prefer to build  up my skills to the point where i can  actually have a genuine interaction with  a person and that the person will not  feel that they're just humoring me but  that they'll actually be  willing to you know converse with me and  i have found that this has worked  uh  you know so uh my advice on output is  continue your input activities  when you already start the output  activities right if you have the  discipline talk to people in meaningful  situations  uh make sure you've prepared yourself so  that you understand what they're saying  don't worry about your mistakes  and when you finish an interaction with  someone regroup  think about  where you had problems and then start  again trying to notice those uh  structures or those vocabulary items in  your input activities but if you can  find an opportunity to totally immerse  yourself in the language where you're  operating in the language either because  you've visited there or you've lined up  three hours a day of skype conversations  you're going to have to do that kind of  thing to really move your output  capabilities up to that level so there  you have it um two answers and uh next  week i am going to answer two other  questions one is do i recommend  studying two languages at once or one at  a time  and what is my take on spaced repetition  systems srs and sentence mining so those  are going to be the questions that i  will answer next week thank you for  listening  you
 

